<FlowDocument xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
              Tag="1.0.22" FontFamily="Segoe UI" FontSize="14" PagePadding="24"
              TextAlignment="Left" Foreground="{DynamicResource NightText}"
              Background="{DynamicResource NightBackground}">
    <Paragraph FontSize="26" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}" Margin="0,0,0,6">
        What's New in 1.0.22
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource NightMuted}" Margin="0,0,0,20">
        Review recent fights in the DPS overlay and see how damage played out over time.
        This release history ships with your installed version and works offline.
    </Paragraph>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Pick any of your last 10 fights in DPS
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            The DPS overlay now has a dropdown at the top. Choose one of your last 10 fights,
            listed newest first with the mob name and start time, to keep the overlay on that
            fight. Choose Current fight (auto) to follow live combat again. Only fights where
            you dealt damage are listed; if a picked fight drops out of the last 10, the overlay
            returns to the current fight. Unlock floating overlays with Ctrl+Shift+L to use it.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Damage timeline in DPS details
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Click the mob name or a combatant in DPS to open details. The new Damage timeline
            tab shows damage over the fight as bars, one second each (wider bars for long fights),
            with quiet seconds left empty. Hover a bar to see its time span, damage, and DPS.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            The Damage types tab shows one row per damage type, such as Melee or each spell and
            DoT. Every row spans the whole fight, and bars run from when that type started
            landing to when it finished, with a mark at each end. A pause of more than 7 seconds
            starts a new bar, so 6-second DoT ticks stay together. The mob name row combines all
            attackers; a combatant row shows only that attacker. Details are snapshots at click time.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Paragraph FontSize="26" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}" Margin="0,24,0,6">
        What's New in 1.0.21
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource NightMuted}" Margin="0,0,0,20">
        Adjust overlay backgrounds in place and pick upgrade targets more easily.
        This release history ships with your installed version and works offline.
    </Paragraph>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Compact background sliders on floating overlays
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Unlock floating overlays with Ctrl+Shift+L to find a small background-opacity
            slider immediately left of each Dock button. Changes apply live to that panel,
            stay synchronized with Settings &gt; General &gt; Overlays &gt; Individual
            overlay appearance and locks, and are saved with its appearance.
            Hover over the slider to see its opacity.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Clicking a slider gives the overlay focus while the thumb captures the drag,
            rather than deliberately leaving the game focused. Click back into the game
            when finished. Locked overlays remain click-through and do not show the slider.
            Docked panels still use Settings for opacity. If hover-only titles are enabled,
            hover over the floating panel to reveal its controls.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Separate search and inventory selection in the mote planner
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Enable Mote upgrade planner under Settings &gt; General &gt; Overlays.
            In Equipment mode, type in the name field to filter a separate inventory
            dropdown below it. Clear the text to see all eligible exported items.
            Picking an item fills its name and sets Current to your best copy's rank,
            as before. You can still type a name to plan without an inventory match.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Spell mode keeps the name field and hides the inventory dropdown because
            inventory exports do not list spells. Upgrade calculations and copy-feeding
            options are unchanged.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Paragraph FontSize="26" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}" Margin="0,0,0,6">
        What's New in 1.0.20
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource NightMuted}" Margin="0,0,0,20">
        Manage timers from their overlays and keep floating panels unobtrusive in game.
        This release history ships with your installed version and works offline.
    </Paragraph>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Track mobs and spells without opening settings
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            In the Spawn Timers overlay, select a checkbox under Track mobs to start
            tracking a named mob observed in the current zone. Uncheck a tracked mob
            to remove its timer. Generic mobs remain available through
            Settings &gt; Spawn timers &gt; Show all mobs.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            In the Spell Timers overlay, uncheck an active spell to stop tracking it,
            or use Known spells to enable or disable a spell with a saved duration.
            These choices are saved. Unchecking a spell applies across its ranks and
            keeps learned durations. Enabling a spell does not cast it or create a
            countdown by itself; timers still depend on logged spell events.
            Unlock floating overlays with Ctrl+Shift+L to use their controls.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Fewer duplicate spell choices
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            The Spell durations settings grid and the overlay's Known spells list
            show only the highest known rank of each spell, rather than listing every
            rank separately. Older ranks' saved learning is kept. Active timers
            still show the rank actually cast and remain individually visible by target.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Reveal overlay titles and borders only on hover
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Open Settings &gt; General &gt; Overlays and enable Hide floating overlay
            titles and borders in game unless hovered. While EverQuest is active,
            floating panels hide their title areas, borders, and decorations until
            the mouse is over them. Header space remains reserved so content does
            not move under the pointer. The option starts off.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Docked panels are unchanged. Locked overlays stay click-through, with
            hover detection updating within half a second. Existing background
            opacity settings still apply. The Alerts overlay also no longer shows
            the No alerts message when there are no active alerts.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Paragraph FontSize="26" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}" Margin="0,0,0,6">
        What's New in 1.0.19
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource NightMuted}" Margin="0,0,0,20">
        Find exaltations by what they do, not just by the item name.
        This release history ships with your installed version and works offline.
    </Paragraph>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Search exaltation effects and locations
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Enable Exaltations in Settings &gt; General &gt; Overlays. It starts docked;
            you can also float it over the game. Run /outputfile inventory in game, then
            search for an effect such as percussion, string, bard instruments, or haste.
            Each card separates the exaltation, its effects, its host item, and its exported
            location. Loose copies and exalt storage are included; Installed only limits
            results to installations confirmed by the export.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Effects and descriptions come from the original item's EQLWiki page and are
            cached locally. Lookups fill in progressively and need internet access for
            uncached items; large inventories can take time. Refresh effects rechecks the
            wiki, not your inventory. Only base-item and effect names are sent to the wiki;
            host items and locations stay local. Click-only effects are not included.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            The game export can omit exaltations installed in Equipment storage.
            Original equipment with supported wiki effects can appear as Expected exaltation
            (unverified), separately from confirmed copies. These are wiki defaults, not proof
            of ownership or current installation; their export-entry numbers are not in-game
            slots. A missing search result does not mean you do not own an exaltation.
            Check the in-game Find Item window, and re-export after moving or swapping items.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            More useful optional usage reporting
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Share usage and error telemetry remains off by default. If enabled under
            Settings &gt; General &gt; Application updates, it now reports bundled theme
            choices, configured overlay visibility and docked or floating placement, and
            fixed feature-action labels. Custom theme contents, inventory, game logs,
            character names, and file paths are not included. Session identifiers are
            temporary, not persistent user or device identifiers.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Placement reports describe configuration, not time actually visible over the game.
            Existing saved telemetry preferences are preserved.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Inventory pane resizing
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            The horizontal splitter between inventory and quest results now resizes both
            panes instead of making the lower pane shrink upward. Both panes retain their
            minimum usable heights.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Paragraph FontSize="26" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}" Margin="0,0,0,6">
        What's New in 1.0.18
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource NightMuted}" Margin="0,0,0,20">
        Keep long NPC encounters together and rebuild tracking data from your logs.
        This release history ships with your installed version and works offline.
    </Paragraph>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            NPC encounters no longer time out
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            NPC compendium encounters now stay open through inactivity, including long gaps
            and restarts. Only a logged death, zoning, login, or a loadout or level change
            closes an encounter. This change does not alter the separate session combat panels.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Missing ending events can merge repeated fights against the same NPC name.
            Previously saved Timed out entries are unchanged. Reopening the same log alone
            does not rebuild old history; use the tracking reset below if you want to reprocess it.
            DoT ticks still add damage rather than direct-spell landings; this is not a complete cast history.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Reset tracking while keeping settings
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Open Settings &gt; General &gt; Tracking data &gt; Reset tracking data....
            Confirm the prompt to save settings and close the app. Open it again to complete
            the reset before tracking stores load, then reprocess the selected log.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            NPC history, resistance observations, learned spell durations and overrides,
            spawn timers and overrides, mote history, and tracked inventory are backed up
            under %AppData%\EqLogOverlay\tracking-backups and cleared from active storage.
            Settings, alert triggers, layout, themes, lookup caches, and original game logs are kept.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Only data present in available logs and inventory exports can be rebuilt.
            Open other log files separately to rebuild their history. Reset failures stop
            startup with an error and retain the request for retry rather than loading partial data.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Paragraph FontSize="26" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}" Margin="0,0,0,6">
        What's New in 1.0.17
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource NightMuted}" Margin="0,0,0,20">
        Usage and error telemetry is now off by default.
        This release history ships with your installed version and works offline.
    </Paragraph>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Choose whether to share telemetry
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            New installs, and settings without a saved telemetry preference, no longer collect
            usage or error events by default. To opt in, open Settings &gt; General &gt;
            Application updates and check Share usage and error telemetry.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Existing saved preferences are preserved, including an enabled setting from an
            earlier version. Uncheck Share usage and error telemetry to stop collecting new
            events; your choice is saved for future starts.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            When enabled, telemetry reports app starts, fixed feature labels, and error types
            and sources. Game and log contents, character and item names, file paths,
            exception messages, and stack traces are not sent. Azure Monitor may process
            standard service and network metadata.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Paragraph FontSize="26" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}" Margin="0,0,0,6">
        What's New in 1.0.16
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource NightMuted}" Margin="0,0,0,20">
        A deeper, VS Code-style charcoal look for Neutral Dark.
        This release history ships with your installed version and works offline.
    </Paragraph>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Darker surfaces, quieter borders
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Neutral Dark now uses deeper charcoal backgrounds, darker controls, subtler
            borders and grid lines, and softer text with restrained blue accents.
            Neutral Light and the other bundled palettes are unchanged.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Open Settings &gt; Appearance &gt; Theme. Follow Windows light/dark mode uses
            the updated palette when Windows app mode is dark. For a fixed choice, uncheck
            that option and select Neutral Dark.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Saved fixed palettes are preserved, including the previous Neutral Dark colors.
            Reselect Neutral Dark from the theme list to use the updated bundled palette.
            Text still meets at least 4.5:1 contrast on opaque surfaces; overlay transparency
            and the game behind it can reduce actual contrast.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Paragraph FontSize="26" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}" Margin="0,0,0,6">
        What's New in 1.0.15
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource NightMuted}" Margin="0,0,0,20">
        A calmer first launch with neutral colors that follow your Windows app appearance.
        This release history ships with your installed version and works offline.
    </Paragraph>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Follow Windows light or dark mode
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            New installs use Neutral Light or Neutral Dark automatically, with simple gray
            surfaces, readable text, and restrained blue accents. Changes to Windows app mode
            also update the theme while the overlay app is running.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Existing saved theme choices are preserved. In Settings &gt; Appearance &gt; Theme,
            check Follow Windows light/dark mode to use automatic switching.
            Windows app mode, rather than the separate Windows shell mode, controls the choice.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Keep a fixed palette if you prefer
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            In Settings &gt; Appearance &gt; Theme, uncheck Follow Windows light/dark mode,
            then choose Neutral Light, Neutral Dark, Halloween, or another bundled theme.
            Import JSON... and the theme editor's Apply &amp; save also select a fixed palette.
            Your choice is saved for future starts; check the Windows option again to resume following it.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Both neutral palettes provide at least 4.5:1 text contrast on their opaque surfaces.
            Floating-overlay transparency and the game behind it can reduce actual contrast.
            Chat channel colors and custom alert colors remain separate from the selected theme.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Paragraph FontSize="26" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}" Margin="0,0,0,6">
        What's New in 1.0.14
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource NightMuted}" Margin="0,0,0,20">
        Smoother log loading and inventory updates, plus a usage and error telemetry preference.
        This release history ships with your installed version and works offline.
    </Paragraph>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Keep using the app while history loads
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Choose a log using General &gt; Log file &gt; Browse... or Choose folder....
            Loading history continues to show progress and partial panel results, while tracker
            snapshots run in the background and panel updates give input time to run.
            Log discovery and floating-overlay visibility checks remain active.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Routine inventory checks, tracker maintenance, and periodic saves resume after the
            reader catches up. Closing the app still saves tracking data. Large logs and busy
            panels can still take time to finish; history does not fire alert triggers.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Less waiting after loading
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Inventory export reconciliation now uses indexed item lookups instead of repeatedly
            scanning your saved inventory. Export parsing and tracker save preparation run in
            the background, and queued loot is applied in short batches so it does not all block
            the interface at once.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Run /outputfile inventory in game to refresh owned counts in Inventory &amp; loot quests,
            Ready to merge, and the Mote upgrade planner. Item upgrade variants are still combined
            for ownership counts, with storage locations and loot newer than the export preserved.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Choose whether to share usage and error events
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            General &gt; Application updates now includes Share usage and error telemetry.
            It is enabled by default and sends app starts, fixed feature labels, and error types
            and sources to Azure Application Insights. Untick it to stop collecting new events;
            your choice is saved for future starts.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Game and log contents, character and item names, file paths, exception messages,
            and stack traces are not sent. Azure Monitor may process standard service and
            network metadata. Errors use custom events rather than detailed crash reports.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Paragraph FontSize="26" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}" Margin="0,0,0,6">
        What's New in 1.0.13
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource NightMuted}" Margin="0,0,0,20">
        The mote upgrade planner is clearer about equipment copies and has a simpler Spell mode.
        This release history is included with your installed version and works offline.
        Older releases are listed below, newest first.
    </Paragraph>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Spell mode is spell-only
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            In Settings, open General &gt; Overlays and enable Mote upgrade planner.
            Choose Spell and type the spell name. The inventory name list and equipment copy,
            feeding, and farming options are hidden, so equipment choices won't be mixed into spell planning.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Understand copy merges and mote carry-over
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            In Equipment mode, choose Equipment, type a name or pick it from your inventory export,
            then set Current and any Points already invested. Use inventory copies, feeding, and farming
            options apply only to equipment. The extra-copy count and farm rank now stay grouped with
            their labels on a separate row.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            What to do now explains when a copy merge alone completes a rank. If motes are also
            listed for that step, they are used first only when the item is about to outgrow them;
            their points carry into the next rank. For example, a +6 copy alone makes a +6 item
            +7, while eligible motes added before that merge carry forward.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Where to see the plan
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Read What to do for the short instructions and missing motes. In Equipment mode,
            Extra copies to farm and at estimate how many additional copies at that rank are needed
            with the motes and copies selected. Show the math opens the detailed tables.
            Spells use motes only; condensing and Void-Touched motes are not included.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Paragraph FontSize="26" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}" Margin="0,24,0,6">
        What's New in 1.0.12
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource NightMuted}" Margin="0,0,0,20">
        Plan upgrades, make alerts easier to notice, and choose a more readable theme.
        This help is included with your installed version and works offline.
    </Paragraph>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Mote upgrade planner
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Work out how to get equipment or a spell from its current +rank to +10.
            In Settings, open General &gt; Overlays and enable Mote upgrade planner.
        </Paragraph>
        <List MarkerStyle="Decimal">
            <ListItem><Paragraph>
                Choose Equipment or Spell, then type a name or pick an item from your inventory export.
                Picking an item selects your highest copy's rank. Set Current and any Points already invested.
            </Paragraph></ListItem>
            <ListItem><Paragraph>
                Use owned motes and Use inventory copies count what you already have.
                Your current item is not a spare. Equipment uses the matching mote tier for each step,
                keeping bigger motes for later ranks.
            </Paragraph></ListItem>
            <ListItem><Paragraph>
                Feed copies with motes first can raise spare copies before you merge them.
                Small motes that your main item has outgrown can still help a lower-rank copy.
                The guide tells you what to feed each copy and in what order to upgrade your item.
            </Paragraph></ListItem>
            <ListItem><Paragraph>
                Choose Farm at to set the +rank of copies you could collect.
                The guide estimates how many more you need with your selected motes and copies.
                Extra copies lets you try a quantity you expect to get; farmed copies can also be fed with motes.
            </Paragraph></ListItem>
            <ListItem><Paragraph>
                Read What to do for the short instructions. Still need lists missing motes;
                the farming estimate is an alternative way to finish. Show the math opens the detailed tables.
                A +10 item is already finished, so it has no upgrade steps.
            </Paragraph></ListItem>
        </List>
        <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource NightMuted}">
            The Motes overlay also shows equipment and spell values beside each tier.
            Inventory counts come from your latest export; run /outputfile inventory in game to refresh them.
            Copies count as their exact displayed +rank, with no assumed partial progress.
            Condensing and Void-Touched motes are not included in the planner.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Clearer alerts and easier trigger editing
        </Paragraph>
        <List>
            <ListItem><Paragraph>
                In Settings &gt; Alert triggers, use Simple text for a plain, case-insensitive match,
                Regex for a pattern, or both. Either match can fire the alert; they are OR, not AND.
            </Paragraph></ListItem>
            <ListItem><Paragraph>
                Trigger cards use multiple rows so the fields are less cramped.
                Choose an alert color with Pick... and select a text effect, such as flash, bounce, or glow.
                Choose None for a still alert.
            </Paragraph></ListItem>
            <ListItem><Paragraph>
                Alerts are centered and wrap to fit the panel. Use Test to try a log line and reveal
                the Alerts panel without waiting for the event in game.
            </Paragraph></ListItem>
        </List>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Relearn spawn and spell timers
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            If a patch or an early break has made a learned timer inaccurate, use Relearn on its row
            in Spawn timers or Spell durations. This clears the learned timing so new observations can
            rebuild it. Existing overrides are kept; clear an override if you want to use learned timing again.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Accessibility themes and a more useful preview
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Open Settings &gt; Appearance and choose High Contrast Dark, High Contrast Light,
            Colorblind Safe (Red-Green), or Colorblind Safe (Blue-Yellow).
            Themes now include overlay heading, secondary, and status text colors.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            The preview shows realistic tables, controls, selected and hovered rows, and overlays,
            so you can see how each color will look before Apply &amp; save.
            Custom alert colors and chat channel colors remain separate from the theme.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
    <Section>
        <Paragraph FontSize="19" FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Pumpkin}">
            Help is now one click away
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Use What's New in the main window's top bar to reopen this page, even with Settings hidden.
            Leave it open beside the app while trying a feature. Close it with the Close button or Escape.
            The README also has new dashboard, floating-overlay, and countdown examples.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            The main dashboard's Always on top checkbox has been removed.
            Floating game overlays still stay on top when they are shown.
        </Paragraph>
    </Section>
</FlowDocument>
